## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying CKNE to practical throughput controls using simple token budgets; ties beginner design to real-world load management.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE to per-tenant rate and queue depth mapping
- Minute-loop throttling and token rebalancing
- Delaying non-critical logs while preserving SLA baseline
- Observability and a simple test plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// CKNE to tokens mapping (simplified)
function tokensForCKNE(ckne, maxRate){
  return Math.max(1, Math.floor(ckne * maxRate));
}
function shouldDrop(log, ckne, queueDepth){
  const threshold = 100 * (1 - ckne);
  return queueDepth > threshold && log.priority !== 'critical';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test with fluctuating CKNE values?
- How would you extend to different log severities and multi-tenant fairness?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses CKNE-driven multi-tenant graph analytics design, focusing on per-tenant prioritization, adaptive traversal, caching, and safe degradation under load. Requires concrete parameters, fairness guarantees, observability hooks, and a test plan.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-routing and depth mapping
- Backpressure and resource reallocation
- Incremental caching and warm/cold budgets
- Telemetry, SLAs, and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
function adjustForCKNE(tenantCKNE, req) {
  const baseDepth = 6;
  if (tenantCKNE < 0.3) {
    req.depth = Math.min(req.depth || baseDepth, 3);
    req.rateLimit = Math.min(req.rateLimit || 200, 100);
  } else if (tenantCKNE > 0.7) {
    req.depth = Math.max(req.depth || baseDepth, 8);
    req.cacheHint = 'warm';
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation loop under bursty CKNE distributions?
- How would you ensure fairness when CKNE changes dynamically?

